. rvfplot, yline(0)
{res}{txt}
{com}. *by observation; we can see that there is a a rectangular spread of the data points ; this fulfills the assumption of equal variance
. 
. * Use Statistical test: Breusch-Pagan / Cook-Weisberg test (estat hettest)
. 
. * H0: Constant variance. p > 0.05 → assumption holds.
. 
. * Overall check
. estat hettest

{txt}Breusch–Pagan/Cook–Weisberg test for heteroskedasticity 
Assumption: Normal error terms
{p 0 11 0}Variable:{space 1}{res:{txt:Fitted values of} {res:bwt}}{p_end}

H0: Constant variance

{ralign 11:chi2({res:1})} = {res}{ralign 6:0.03}
{txt}{ralign 11:Prob > chi2} = {res}{ralign 6:0.8660}
{txt}
{com}. 
. * per variable;
. 
. estat hettest age

{txt}Breusch–Pagan/Cook–Weisberg test for heteroskedasticity 
Assumption: Normal error terms
{p 0 11 0}Variable:{space 1}{res:age}{p_end}

H0: Constant variance

{ralign 11:chi2({res:1})} = {res}{ralign 6:5.21}
{txt}{ralign 11:Prob > chi2} = {res}{ralign 6:0.0224}
{txt}
{com}. *Breusch-Pagan / Cook-Weisberg test for age is signficant; hence null hypotheis of constant variance is rejected
. 
